in this part  of the democratic republic of the congo  medical care arrives by water  the rivers are the country's roads the  ambulance boat is part of a conservation  project  and was funded by the worldwide fund for  nature to help people  in the hungary nature reserve didier  mazongo is the project's director  we are now in the nature reserve in the  ingary triangle where there  are quite a number of villages the aim  of this project  is to improve the living conditions of  the population  while helping them preserve its  biodiversity  and that's sorely needed the rainforest  in central africa  actually has one of the world's highest  levels of biodiversity  but the forests rivers and sandbanks  here are empty  civil war poverty and hardship have  almost wiped out the fauna  people hunt anything that's edible  didier mazongo says that while the  nature reserve is covered in water  almost all year long  there are still animals here but they're  very shy  we're now in the heart of the reserve  with its specific character as a swamp  forest  which plays a crucial role in  maintaining the water supply  and preserving biodiversity you can  still find rare antelope species  and even hippopotami in this river  the people along the rivers live mainly  from fishing  most use mosquito nets for their catch  hardly any have broad mesh nets as a  result  the fish stocks are severely depleted  many species are threatened with  extinction  didier mazongo and his colleague albert  bakonza assessed the situation at a  village market  how big do these fish normally get  as long as the poverty here remains so  great it will be hard to keep the fauna  along the congo from disappearing  so the ungiri triangle project is a sort  of noah's ark  from which one day animals might  repopulate the nearly empty forests and  rivers  at least that's what didier mazongo and  his fellow campaigners hope
 

